// THE SECRET OF SECRETS (SIRR AL-ASRĀR; LATIN SECRETUM SECRETORUM), in Ismail
// Ali's English of the Arabic, from Steele's edition of Roger Bacon's
// Secretum secretorum (1920), discourse by discourse.
//
// The text comes from secretum-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/steele-secretum.py from the OCRs of three printed copies of
// the book, compared word by word, with the readings the OCRs could not
// settle checked on the page images. The English is a translation of one
// Arabic manuscript, Gotha 1869 (Steele's A); Steele's footnotes, which give
// the variants of the other manuscripts, and his marginal notes are left out.
//
// The book is a letter of Aristotle to Alexander in ten discourses, after a
// translator's preface and Aristotle's own preface; those two prefaces are
// kept together as "before Discourse I" (arab.secretum.0). The units are the
// ten discourses. The long ones (I–IV, IX, X) hold no text of their own:
// beneath them, the archive's own parts, cut at Steele's headings or at a
// change of subject. Discourses V–VIII are kept whole.
//
// Philip of Tripoli's Latin, which Steele prints in full (pp. 1–172), is
// public domain but is not transcribed here; its Emerald Tablet is already in
// the archive (arab.tab.secretum). Manzalaoui's Middle English versions (1977)
// and Badawi's Arabic text (1954) are in copyright and are cited, never
// quoted.
//
// Nothing is marked verified. The Arabic recensions differ in length and
// order, and the ten discourses of this one have not been checked against a
// stated inventory of the book (inventoryVerified stays false).

